@Service
public class AuthService {
@Service annotation
Marks this class as a Spring service bean. Functionally identical to @Component but @Service communicates intent — 
this class contains business logic, not a web controller or database repository. Spring detects it during component scan 
and creates one instance managed in the application context.

  @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @Autowired
    private PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder;

    @Autowired
    private AuthenticationManager authManager;

    @Autowired
    private JwtUtil jwtUtil;
Dependencies injected by Spring
Four dependencies the AuthService needs:

userRepository → to save new users and look up existing ones from MySQL
passwordEncoder → BCryptPasswordEncoder to hash passwords on register and verify on login
authManager → Spring Security's AuthenticationManager to validate username + password during login
jwtUtil → to generate the JWT token after successful login

Spring injects all of these automatically. We never write "new UserRepository()" — Spring manages the lifecycle.